this came about when I met Kevin I'd  been invited to be a mentor at a retreat  for extreme sports athletes what Kevin  would call action sports athletes what I  call extreme sports athletes and it was  a retreat and designed to inspire  athletes to use their platform for  social change and we were squidding my  film wasteland and at the back of my  mind was this idea extreme sports that's  a really interesting world and I don't  know what I make of it because when I  watch those sports I find myself being  very compelled my eyeballs are sucked  out of my head and I want to watch them  but I'm so worried about them athletes  so I happened to meet Kevin and  instantly was struck by him of course as  you've seen he just struck me as a  incredible charismatic star with an  incredible story now this was after the  crash this was Kevin's very first outing  after the crash a 2010 and kevin was  very early in his recovery he kept  reintroducing himself to me and he  couldn't watch my phone wasteland and he  was really early in his recovery and to  start with I thought my gosh what an  amazing young man but I didn't think of  making a film I guess for two reasons  partly it seemed like the story was over  and then as I observed Kevin I realized  that the story wasn't over at all the  story was about to get in fact extremely  interesting because I observed Kevin  wanting to get back and and keep up with  his colleagues who were all doing crazy  dangerous stuff around the place and and  I thought my gosh what is it to be have  this thing that you do taken away from  you and so suddenly and dramatically and  and is he going to dig deep what's going  to happen to him I wanted to find out  and you saw him those family scenes and  just everything they welcome Lucy and  the camera crew and everybody just in  and kind of really gave a very cool  perspective so I was so lucky you know  even something as big as having cameras  in at your Thanksgiving dinner you know  they just are so open and just welcoming  make Lucy feel like a part of the family  and I think that's really kind of what  allowed this you know to be you know  come across so amazingly and there was  only ever two or three of us if there's  if it's simple our record the sound  myself if it's a little more complicated  I'll get somebody better than me but I  really like being small and intimate  otherwise I just don't think it's as  effective and and I have lots of tricks  and tips I have lots of things like I  don't like to give people the  opportunity to cut I usually say don't  worry let's film everything and we can  figure out afterwards what to use and I  have tricks but I have to say my main  trick is being extremely picky about who  I work with and this family was  incredible as you've seen and I think  also they have a philosophy that if  there's a problem or a disability you  shouldn't be ashamed of it and it's good  to share it and they have this courage  and honesty that you can't no amount of  brilliant documentary directing is as  good as picking my dad had some of that  film at some of that footage from the  very early days and then growing up kind  of there was always a camera around me  and I'd always been filmed and so there  was a ton of footage and you know  luckily for me my family hadn't always  been filmed and they just open arms  welcome Lucy and the whole crew into our  family and allowed us to be filmed but  for me it was just normal because I've  been filmed my whole life's and luckily  for the movie we were able to get all  that footage talking to someone last  night and it's like the main thing for  me is that I'm still recovering and you  know I'm still fully in the middle of  this and it's so hard just cuz it's been  three and a half three and a half years  but still my brain is you know still so  much getting better and I'm still kind  of you know seeing changes and it's very  slow and it's kind of very hard but you  know that's the coolest part that it's  like right now I'm my brain is still  getting better and along with that I'm  doing announcing and then trying to do  some public speaking and then been  cruising around with this amazing movie  showing this and kind of letting people  see what Lucy you know put together so  I've been busy and life's been really  fun for me  it's different now because I'm not  competing anymore but it's still fun I  think Kevin's story dramatizes how high  the stakes are for these athletes and  what is going on in this world of  extreme sports that has only ever been  shown through these glamorous films that  only ever show how you know gorgeous and  snazzy it all is and we wanted to really  so truthfully share Kevin's experience  and his family's experience which is  surprisingly rare that you know there's  so many sports films but not something  like this that's been done before and I  think it does raise a lot of questions  about whether the conversation about  safety has kept pace with this thrilling  evolution in the sport in in in our  lifetimes I'd like to thank these guys  mom and dad and Dave Dave especially  coming oh they want sup come on up there  thank you guys
 

